    {
      "id": 14,
      "title": "How To Read Slow Refreshes",
      "type": "text",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 56
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Interpretation guide\n- **High RESET ACK duration**: consumer was slow to ACK the RESET control message. This usually points to consumer connectivity, consumer startup, or control-path delay rather than broker replay throughput.\n- **High Pure Replay Duration**: most of the refresh time was spent replaying stored data. If **Broker CPU** or **Broker Disk I/O** are high during the same window, the bottleneck is likely broker/storage side. If broker resources stay calm but one consumer is slow, the bottleneck is more likely consumer-side processing or network.\n- **High READY ACK wait**: replay finished, but the consumer took time to send READY_ACK. That usually means a consumer-side finalize/ack delay.\n- **High broker network send duration / backpressure / send failures** with calm disk and storage metrics points to broker-side network pressure.\n- **Current Replay Gap**: shows who is still behind right now during an active refresh."
      }
    },
